WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:02.810
- [Narrator] This is like
a giant vacuum cleaner

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:03.950
for the atmosphere,

00:00:03.950 --> 00:00:05.010
and it could be one answer

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:06.988
to slashing Earth's carbon emissions.

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:10.793
- We take it out of the air
so we reverse climate change.

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.380
- [Narrator] This is
same as cleaning up some

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:15.880
of the 33 billion tons of carbon dioxide

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:17.760
that humans will release
into the atmosphere

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.180
by the end of the year.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.910
And the International Energy Agency

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:23.143
says it's the highest concentration ever.

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:25.450
- [Crowd] Save our planet.

00:00:25.450 --> 00:00:27.260
- [Narrator] Companies are
increasingly facing pressure

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:28.680
from consumers and investors

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:30.640
to reduce their carbon footprints.

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:33.030
So some are turning to the
carbon capture industry

00:00:33.030 --> 00:00:34.330
that's forecasted to be worth

00:00:34.330 --> 00:00:37.613
approximately $15 billion by 2027.

00:00:38.730 --> 00:00:41.550
Microsoft and Audi are
investing in this technology,

00:00:41.550 --> 00:00:44.401
while Coca-Cola is purchasing
the carbon that's caught.

00:00:44.401 --> 00:00:46.540
- We believe it's one of the solutions

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:48.100
to fight climate change.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.490
- [Narrator] But some
climate experts doubt

00:00:49.490 --> 00:00:51.580
the technology can have
a meaningful impact

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:52.520
on the environment.

00:00:52.520 --> 00:00:54.330
- Probably one of the
biggest challenges here

00:00:54.330 --> 00:00:57.440
is having captured it,
what can you do with it?

00:00:57.440 --> 00:00:58.730
- [Narrator] We visit one of the largest

00:00:58.730 --> 00:01:01.530
open air carbon capture
facilities on the planet

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:04.170
to see how it absorbs, then sells its CO2,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:06.620
and how that could help
companies become cleaner.

00:01:08.120 --> 00:01:09.240
A lot of companies are trying

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:11.460
to remove carbon dioxide from the air.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:12.930
Some want to capture it directly

00:01:12.930 --> 00:01:15.370
by attaching filters
on trucks and factories

00:01:15.370 --> 00:01:18.190
or by installing ventilation
systems in leading rooms.

00:01:18.190 --> 00:01:20.870
- Here we basically have air being pumped

00:01:20.870 --> 00:01:22.603
inside this chamber.

00:01:22.603 --> 00:01:24.577
- [Narrator] But Climeworks
takes carbon dioxide

00:01:24.577 --> 00:01:26.950
directly out of the atmosphere.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:31.950
- We blow air through this
collector using a ventilator.

00:01:32.220 --> 00:01:35.260
The CO2 sticks inside this box,

00:01:35.260 --> 00:01:36.793
and stays there.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.510
- [Narrator] Nathalie Casas

00:01:38.510 --> 00:01:40.430
leads Climeworks' research program.

00:01:40.430 --> 00:01:41.640
She says this facility

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:43.330
is made up of 30 ventilators

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:45.360
that can pull in about 1,500 tons

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:48.910
of carbon dioxide out of
the atmosphere every year.

00:01:48.910 --> 00:01:51.990
- Inside the collector,
we have a filter material.

00:01:51.990 --> 00:01:56.287
This filter material is able to catch CO2.

00:01:56.287 --> 00:01:57.360
(dramatic music)

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:59.010
- [Narrator] Casas says
the biggest challenge

00:01:59.010 --> 00:02:01.640
is making it affordable to set up and use.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:04.140
Building and installing just
one of these ventilators here

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:08.010
at this facility costs
as much as $220,000.

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.010
And it also needs a lot of electricity.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:12.050
So to clean up the amount of CO2 someone

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.470
in the US would typically
produce in about a month,

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:16.250
that's everything from commuting to work

00:02:16.250 --> 00:02:18.620
to consuming food and watching TV,

00:02:18.620 --> 00:02:20.610
the company says it would
cost around anywhere

00:02:20.610 --> 00:02:23.220
from 600 to nearly $900.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.730
- The target, of course,
is to bring costs down,

00:02:25.730 --> 00:02:27.920
to bring the energy requirements down,

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:30.596
and to make it easily scalable.

00:02:30.596 --> 00:02:32.190
- [Narrator] So Casas has been working

00:02:32.190 --> 00:02:33.530
on designing larger plants

00:02:33.530 --> 00:02:35.420
that would use renewable energy sources

00:02:35.420 --> 00:02:37.100
and require less steel.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:38.480
But one of the most promising ways

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:40.940
to offset the cost has
been tapping directly

00:02:40.940 --> 00:02:42.890
into the captured CO2.

00:02:42.890 --> 00:02:44.920
Climeworks stores the
carbon dioxide it collects

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:46.140
into these tanks.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:48.360
Then it sells it to this Coca-Cola factory

00:02:48.360 --> 00:02:50.260
that produces bottled water.

00:02:50.260 --> 00:02:51.980
Here, recycled carbon dioxide

00:02:51.980 --> 00:02:54.113
is injected in the water to make it fizzy.

00:02:55.250 --> 00:02:57.930
- 30% of our production is done

00:02:57.930 --> 00:03:01.220
with the recycled CO2 from Climeworks.

00:03:01.220 --> 00:03:02.680
- [Narrator] Patrick Wittweiler oversees

00:03:02.680 --> 00:03:05.180
the carbon dioxide project at Coca-Cola.

00:03:05.180 --> 00:03:07.490
He says early on, there were challenges,

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:08.920
like turning the carbon dioxide

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:11.033
into the right kind of liquified form.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:15.170
- We run a lot of tests and validations

00:03:15.170 --> 00:03:17.980
just to be sure that the quality remains

00:03:17.980 --> 00:03:19.424
on the highest level.

00:03:19.424 --> 00:03:21.980
- [Narrator] Wittweiler
says using recycled CO2

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:23.700
is significantly more expensive

00:03:23.700 --> 00:03:25.110
than the commercial kind.

00:03:25.110 --> 00:03:27.150
But Coca-Cola is committed to lowering

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:28.890
its environmental footprint.

00:03:28.890 --> 00:03:31.010
Over time, he expects
the costs to diminish

00:03:31.010 --> 00:03:33.180
and that would allow the
company to expand the use

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:35.370
of recycled CO2 from bottled water

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:37.180
to other drinks like Coke.

00:03:37.180 --> 00:03:38.370
- We're testing it,

00:03:38.370 --> 00:03:42.114
and we look into further expansions.

00:03:42.114 --> 00:03:43.550
- [Narrator] Making drinks fizzy

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.120
is just one way to use recycled CO2.

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:48.730
Carbon capture startups
have also been experimenting

00:03:48.730 --> 00:03:51.483
with creating fuel,
toothpaste and plastic.

00:03:52.460 --> 00:03:54.280
Climeworks also has another way to profit

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:56.220
from the carbon it captures.

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.640
The startup sells what it
calls a carbon removal service

00:03:59.640 --> 00:04:02.610
so that individuals or
companies like Shopify and Audi

00:04:02.610 --> 00:04:04.730
can offset their own emissions.

00:04:04.730 --> 00:04:06.910
- You are flying to Mallorca,

00:04:06.910 --> 00:04:08.960
but I know it's not so
good for the environment.

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.230
I really wanna compensate,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.500
you can buy carbon dioxide.

00:04:12.500 --> 00:04:13.530
- [Narrator] This part of the industry

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:14.960
is booming and fast evolving

00:04:14.960 --> 00:04:17.030
as regulators and climate analysts debate

00:04:17.030 --> 00:04:19.130
how to best price this type of service.

00:04:19.130 --> 00:04:19.963
- There are better ways

00:04:19.963 --> 00:04:23.710
of capturing the carbon
dioxide than they're using.

00:04:23.710 --> 00:04:25.560
- [Narrator] Sandra Kentish is a professor

00:04:25.560 --> 00:04:27.040
at the University of Melbourne

00:04:27.040 --> 00:04:29.310
who tracks and compares
carbon capture systems

00:04:29.310 --> 00:04:30.550
around the world.

00:04:30.550 --> 00:04:32.430
She says it's harder to absorb CO2

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:33.410
from the atmosphere,

00:04:33.410 --> 00:04:35.930
rather than a direct source of pollution.

00:04:35.930 --> 00:04:38.420
- The CO2 concentration in the atmosphere

00:04:38.420 --> 00:04:42.330
is only about .04%.

00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:44.570
Cement production, as an example,

00:04:44.570 --> 00:04:47.690
CO2 concentration's around 30%.

00:04:47.690 --> 00:04:50.810
So it's hundreds of times easier

00:04:50.810 --> 00:04:54.250
to capture from something
like a cement flue gas

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:57.591
than it will be directly
from the atmosphere.

00:04:57.591 --> 00:04:58.450
(dramatic music)

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.910
- [Narrator] This inefficiency means

00:04:59.910 --> 00:05:02.190
that to capture 1% of
the world's emissions

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:04.250
in a year, Climeworks would have to build

00:05:04.250 --> 00:05:06.860
around 800,000 ventilators.

00:05:06.860 --> 00:05:08.800
But Casas says the company's technology

00:05:08.800 --> 00:05:10.100
can still make a difference.

00:05:10.100 --> 00:05:12.460
- Unfortunately, the mass is already here.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:14.600
So we need to take out CO2.

00:05:14.600 --> 00:05:17.410
- [Narrator] And Climeworks
plans to take out more CO2

00:05:17.410 --> 00:05:19.130
with a near facility in Iceland

00:05:19.130 --> 00:05:21.560
that was built with some
funding from Microsoft.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:22.960
When it opens in September,

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:24.630
the company says the plant will be able

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.350
to absorb about four times more

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:27.740
than its Swiss facility.

00:05:27.740 --> 00:05:30.600
But this time, the carbon
dioxide won't be recycled.

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:31.880
It'll be injected in the ground

00:05:31.880 --> 00:05:33.830
and turned into a rock.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:35.530
- These are these white crystals.

00:05:35.530 --> 00:05:39.690
So this CO2 is actual
CO2, which was in the air.

00:05:39.690 --> 00:05:41.080
- [Narrator] And this kind of evolution

00:05:41.080 --> 00:05:42.869
is how Climeworks hopes its technology

00:05:42.869 --> 00:05:45.690
will become cheaper so
that it can clean the air

00:05:45.690 --> 00:05:47.600
for companies searching for new ways

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:49.179
to reduce their own emissions.

00:05:49.179 --> 00:05:51.929
(dramatic music)

